# Validate a user-supplied lawful source installation

Validate a lawful local installation in place without copying it into the
repository and without ever writing, replacing, renaming, deleting, or patching
anything inside the original installation directory.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Accept a selected installation folder, a pasted/typed path, or a dropped
  `Simpsons.exe` and resolve it to one candidate game root.
- [x] Require the exact canonical source identities owned by the dedicated
  minimum-source TODO, including `Simpsons.exe` and `Simpsons.ico`; keep
  `uninst.ico` optional.
- [x] Require the English source identities needed by the canonical base game.
- [x] Treat other supported original languages as optional source that can later
  be exported as language mods rather than required base-game evidence.
- [x] Run a fast public-manifest gate for user feedback, then run deeper
  deterministic structural/provenance validation before any reconstruction.
- [x] Never let editing a public JSON manifest turn source that fails the deep
  validator into accepted source.
- [ ] Apply the finalized source-similarity policy only after its 45-55%
  planning
  window has been empirically calibrated; similarity never substitutes for
  exact mandatory files or required source bytes.
- [x] Keep every validator operation read-only against the original game path.
- [x] Report why validation failed without exposing private absolute paths in
  tracked evidence, release artifacts, or telemetry.
- [x] Update README whenever the user-visible source requirements change.

## Dependencies

Depends on exact minimum identities, canonical English authority, and the
public-safe reconstruction/similarity policy.

## Evidence

The lightweight release will point at the user's existing lawful installation;
normal players will not be instructed to copy the complete game into the SHAR
repository or release directory.

The minimum validator no longer requires SHAR policy files inside the source
installation. `ValidateManifest::execute_with_manifest` receives the source tree
and the SHAR-owned minimum manifest as separate inputs. An integration fixture
validates a source directory with no `manifest/` child and proves the validator
does not create one. The `validate-game` CLI accepts an optional explicit
manifest path while preserving the developer `game/` default.

`tools/build/adapter-inbound/check.py` now accepts `--game` as either an
installation directory
or `Simpsons.exe`, resolves both to the same source root, and invokes the
validator
with the repository-owned `game/manifest/game.jsonl` as a separate argument. The
saved local preflight reuses that selected source during revalidation. Focused
packaging tests cover directory/executable resolution, wrong-file rejection,
private-path-safe missing-source diagnostics, separate validator inputs, and
saved-source evidence.

The canonical manifest itself already enforces exact `Simpsons.exe`,
`Simpsons.ico`, English `README.rtf`, `dialog.rcf`, `srr2.E`, and `srr2.txt`.
It keeps `uninst.ico` plus other official-language sources optional.

The build dependency bootstrap now publishes both `validate-game` and
`validate-source-deep` with binary and source-closure SHA-256 evidence. Build
preflight runs the exact manifest gate first and only then invokes the deep
P3D/RCF/RSD/RMV audit, recording both results in saved evidence. A stale deep
validator is rejected when its source closure changes, and focused packaging
tests prove the ordered fast-then-deep call sequence. The deep validator's Rust
contract tests prove malformed source failures do not modify source payloads and
do not disclose the selected private path.

Build preflight also redacts the selected source root from manifest and deep
validator stdout, stderr, and execution errors before those messages are
reported or stored as validation summaries. Regression tests preserve useful
shortfall details while requiring the private root to become
`<selected-source>`, and a real `validate-game` failure fixture confirms the
wrapper does not echo its absolute source path.

The root README is synchronized with the current exact minimum-source authority:
it names the six mandatory base files, keeps `uninst.ico` optional, and explains
that other official-language evidence is exported as language mods instead of
becoming mandatory base input.

The remaining unchecked criteria are intentionally open: the final `src/user/`
GUI/drop surface and empirically calibrated similarity admission.
